From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sultan Alsawaf <sultan@kerneltoast.com>
Date: Tue, 26 Aug 2025 22:23:37 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] i2c: designware: Always poll to lower interrupt overhead and
 save energy

On a laptop with an i9-13900H (Asus Zenbook UX6404), resting a finger on
the 140 Hz touchpad produces thousands of i2c interrupts per second,
thereby preventing the CPU from entering any package-level C-states. As a
result, the lost package C-state time causes the package power to skyrocket
to 8 W, from a mere 0.5 W before touching the touchpad.

By busy polling for i2c transaction completion instead of using an
interrupt, power consumption during the above scenario improves
significantly, with package power measuring at 3 W for a total of 5 W of
savings.

Therefore, always poll for better energy efficiency.

Note that on AMD it is better to keep the usleep_range() in the poll loop
in i2c_dw_wait_transfer(), whereas on Intel it is more energy efficient to
omit it.

Signed-off-by: Sultan Alsawaf <sultan@kerneltoast.com>

diff --git a/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-designware-common.c b/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-designware-common.c
index e4dfa2ec58bb76e0f02e6ee9b840a37b0cc74dac..448af232395aa7422a260c8ba7326ee301635b33 100644
--- a/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-designware-common.c
+++ b/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-designware-common.c
@@ -892,6 +892,9 @@ int i2c_dw_probe(struct dw_i2c_dev *dev)
 
 	device_set_node(&dev->adapter.dev, dev_fwnode(dev->dev));
 
+	/* Always poll since it's more energy efficient than the spammy IRQ */
+	dev->flags |= ACCESS_POLLING;
+
 	ret = i2c_dw_init_regmap(dev);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
diff --git a/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-designware-master.c b/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-designware-master.c
index 7a301c8b604ef448f24a57c278e8e8fb44d5f438..12e1d6d374ca315a56b11fff3730b3e82763347c 100644
--- a/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-designware-master.c
+++ b/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-designware-master.c
@@ -715,6 +715,11 @@ irqreturn_t i2c_dw_isr_master(struct dw_i2c_dev *dev)
 
 static int i2c_dw_wait_transfer(struct dw_i2c_dev *dev)
 {
+	/*
+	 * It saves more energy on Intel to omit the usleep_range(). This is not
+	 * the case on AMD.
+	 */
+	const bool no_sleep = boot_cpu_data.x86_vendor == X86_VENDOR_INTEL;
 	unsigned long timeout = dev->adapter.timeout;
 	unsigned int stat;
 	int ret;
@@ -731,7 +736,7 @@ static int i2c_dw_wait_transfer(struct dw_i2c_dev *dev)
 			stat = i2c_dw_read_clear_intrbits(dev);
 			if (stat)
 				i2c_dw_process_transfer(dev, stat);
-			else
+			else if (!no_sleep)
 				/* Try save some power */
 				usleep_range(3, 25);
 		} while (time_before(jiffies, timeout));
